{"parentUuid":"e2f1ad29-8c5f-49d1-9ac9-c4109885b6be","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"a08abe0c37f20c6b8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LeWj55qcx1HhTSH9CkR3sR","type":"tool_result","content":"# CP-SAT Solver Scheduling and Lower Bound Search\n\nBased on the provided content, there is **no explicit information** about `lb_tree_search`, `extra_subsolvers`, or detailed guidance on subsolver scheduling in the document provided.\n\n## What the Document Does Cover\n\nThe material describes the general parallel subsolver approach:\n\n**Portfolio Architecture:**\n\"Several full subsolvers, each using a unique strategy, are run across different threads\" with strategies including \"more linearized models,\" \"aggressive restarts,\" and \"focus on either the lower or upper bound.\"\n\n**Subsolver Types:**\nThe document mentions two categories operating during solution search:\n- Complete subsolvers competing to find optimal solutions\n- Incomplete subsolvers applying local search heuristics like Large Neighborhood Search once a feasible solution exists\n\n**Scheduling Concept:**\nIncomplete workers employ \"a Round Robin approach\" when iterating through strategies, though this specifically references LNS iterations rather than subsolver selection.\n\n## Limitation\n\nThe source material does not discuss `lb_tree_search` parameters, the `extra_subsolvers` configuration option, or specific guidance on when to enable lower-bound-focused tree search strategies. For this information, you would need to consult the CP-SAT parameter documentation or source code referenced in the learning path section."}]},"uuid":"857abd33-5127-4eb4-a727-c894083acecf","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:39.101Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e2f1ad29-8c5f-49d1-9ac9-c4109885b6be","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","versio